•	Worker starts a Reddit operation.
•	Retry policy decides whether an attempt is allowed.
•	Rate limiter waits if the provider is being paced.
•	The HTTP request starts.
•	A normal 2xx response succeeds.
•	A 429 response can use Retry-After before retrying.
•	A 5xx or timeout can use exponential backoff.
•	Permanent errors such as 401, 403, 404, or invalid input fail without retries.
•	Cancellation stops the process instead of starting another attempt.


Defines a simple Wait(ctx) rule for outbound integrations.
Each provider gets its own limiter, so one provider does not throttle another.
OAuth and Reddit API requests share the same Reddit pacing rule.
Understands HTTP 429 and uses the server's Retry-After value when provided.
Temporary failures wait longer between retries, up to a maximum.
Centralizes which errors can retry and how many attempts are allowed.
Cancellation stops waiting and prevents another retry.
Retries are visible in safe logs and low-cardinality Prometheus metrics.
Checks immediate requests, pacing, concurrency, cancellation, and source independence.
The real Reddit worker uses the shared Reddit limiter for authentication and API calls.
A temporary Reddit failure does not permanently break the worker; a later run can recover.
Formatting, tests, race detection, vet, lint, and integration checks must all pass.
